#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fx-cloud: run heavy commands in Vercel Sandboxes, and keep everything else on this Mac.

Each agent (fx, Claude Code, Codex, or a plain shell) gets its own sandbox for each folder it works
in. The first command starts one from the base snapshot; later commands reuse it. A sandbox ends
when the agent runs `fx-cloud done`, after `idle_minutes` without commands, or soon after the
agent process that owns it ends. FXC_OWNER names another owner, for example a script.

Set up once:
  fx-cloud init [--team TEAM] [--project PROJECT] [--prefix PREFIX]
                             choose the Vercel team and project, and write the settings file
  fx-cloud base [--no-warm] [--no-cpu-seeds] [--from SANDBOX]
                             build the base snapshot that every sandbox starts from (`setup`
                             does the same); run it again after you change tools or skills
  fx-cloud login             optional sign-ins in the base: GitHub, and fx if you use it

Run from the project folder:
  fx-cloud run [--force] [--include PATH]... <command>
                                     copy this folder to the sandbox, then run the command there;
                                     output streams back and the exit code is the sandbox's
  fx-cloud run --no-push <command>   run without copying, for example to install a tool
  fx-cloud push [--force]            only copy this folder to the sandbox
  fx-cloud pull --patch [--apply]    the sandbox copy's changes as a patch; --apply applies it here
  fx-cloud pull <path> [<dest>]      copy a file or folder from the sandbox copy to the Mac
  fx-cloud macos-build [zig args]    build a Zig project's macOS binary in the sandbox and put it
                                     in this worktree's zig-out/bin
  fx-cloud agent [--resume ID] [--include PATH]... "<task>"
                                     hand a task to fx (`agent_command`) in the sandbox copy;
                                     prints its answer, its session ID, and the files it changed

The copy lives at the same path in the sandbox.
- A git worktree copy matches the Mac: the commit, uncommitted and new files. Ignored files stay on
  the Mac unless a command needs them: name them with --include, or list them in .fx-cloud-include.
  The Mac is the source of truth: a copy that the sandbox changed is never overwritten without
  --force; pull its changes first.
- A folder outside git gets a one-way copy, up to 200 MB, without build and dependency folders.
  Sandbox changes there can be overwritten, and pull --patch does not work.
A command that runs keeps its sandbox alive, however long it takes.

Sandboxes:
  fx-cloud done [--all]      remove this agent's sandbox for this folder (--all: every folder)
  fx-cloud ls                list the agent sandboxes, their owners and folders
  fx-cloud gc [--all] [--dry-run]
                             remove sandboxes whose agent or folder is gone (--all: every one)
  fx-cloud shell [--base]    open a shell in this agent's sandbox for this folder (or the base)
  fx-cloud extend <time>     add time to this agent's sandbox, for example 1h
  fx-cloud status            show the settings, the base snapshot and the agent sandboxes
  fx-cloud base --remove     remove the base sandbox and its snapshots

Settings: ~/.config/fx-cloud/config.json (FXC_CONFIG_DIR moves the folder). FXC_TEAM, FXC_PROJECT,
FXC_PREFIX, FXC_VCPUS, FXC_IDLE_MINUTES and FXC_MAX_AGENTS override it. State is in
~/.cache/fx-cloud (FXC_STATE_DIR).
"""
